  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nA strong upper ridge remains over much of the Northern and Central \nPlains. An upper high continues to be over portions of the Central \nand Southern Plains. A weak cold front extends down from a surface \nlow in Wisconsin through central Iowa into southern Nebraska. In our \narea, dewpoints range from the lower 60s in central KS to the lower \n70s in southeast KS. High temperatures have exceeded 100 degrees \nacross much of our area with areas along and west of I-135 between \n105 to 110. Heat indices have risen above 105 and are likely to \nexceed 110 degrees into early this evening. The warmest values are \nexpected in central KS with indices up to 115 degrees possible. An \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in effect through 9 PM tonight.\n\nThis evening into tonight, the aforementioned cold front will \ncontinue to move south into northern Kansas likely along I-70. The \nupper ridge will breakdown slightly, allowing a weak shortwave \ntrough to eject out into the Central Plains tonight. This front will \nlikely become the focus for storm chances during the overnight hours \nand into Tuesday morning in our area as the weak shortwave continues \nto slide east across the region. Storms will initiate over eastern \nColorado, western Nebraska, and northwest Kansas this evening into \ntonight near the frontal boundary. These storms will slowly move \neast into central KS likely around/after 4 AM. The main hazard with \nthese storms and showers will be strong gusty winds as DCAPE values \nexceed 1400 J/kg especially as it is possible that the complex of \nstorms dissipate as they move into our area given the warm 700 mb \ntemperatures. Showers and storms are possible for much of the day \nTuesday especially in portions of central and eastern KS as 700 mb \nWAA persists. The cold front will make its way towards southern KS \nby Tuesday afternoon, resulting in slightly cooler temperatures \nacross the region compared to the last few days. Highs will range \nfrom the mid 90s in central KS to near 100 in southern KS. Dewpoints \nare expected to be in the upper 60s to lower 70s. This will result \nin another warm day with heat indices near 100 in central KS and \nvalues between 105-110 in southern KS. As such, the Heat Advisory is \nnow in effect for south-central and southeast KS through 8 PM \nTuesday.  \n\nAdditional rain chances will be possible late Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning as another weak shortwave trough slides across the \nregion with 700 mb WAA aiding in shower and storm chances. Storms \nare expected to develop off the High Plains again Tuesday night and \nslowly progress eastward towards central KS during the overnight \nhours. Again given the very warm 700 mb temperatures, storms are \nlikely to dissipate as they move into our area. These chances are \nlikely to linger across portions of eastern KS on Wednesday morning \nwith chances decreasing towards Wednesday afternoon. Even with the \nupper ridge breaking down slightly over the Central Plains, high \ntemperatures will remain in the 90s across the region for both \nWednesday and Thursday afternoon. Weak northwesterly flow aloft will \ncontinue allowing embedded shortwave troughs to eject and slide \nacross the region. This will result in rain chances Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning, and then likely again Thursday night into \nFriday. At this time, severe weather chances with any of this \nactivity continue to remain very low. The upper-level ridge axis \nwill shift westward towards the Central Great Basin and Desert \nSouthwest late in the work week. Guidance suggests near normal or \nslightly below normal high temperatures towards the end of the week \nwith highs in the 80s to low 90s.",
